{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 9, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by rising Islamic power under Saladin. His lineage connected him to the highest nobility since his father was Raymond II who died under mysterious circumstances that left the young count to inherit a fragile state bordering hostile territories. Raymond spent part of his early captivity in Aleppo where he learned Arabic language skills and developed a deep understanding of Muslim culture that would later define his diplomatic approach toward neighboring emirs and sultans.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nHis time as a prisoner shaped his worldview significantly because he realized that military confrontation alone could not secure the future of the Crusader states without strategic alliances or negotiated truces. This perspective often put him at odds with more aggressive factions within the kingdom who favored immediate warfare over diplomacy. He eventually secured his release through a ransom deal and returned to Tripoli to rebuild his domain while maintaining cautious relations with Nur ad-Din and later Saladin.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nRaymond acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy yet displayed remarkable courage despite his debilitating illness. The count sought to guide the young king toward policies that would preserve the realm through careful negotiation rather than reckless expansion. Tensions arose between Raymond and Guy of Lusignan who married Sibylla and sought the throne through forceful means. Raymond opposed Guy vigorously because he believed that such leadership would lead to disaster for the entire Christian presence in the Holy Land. His political maneuvering included forming alliances with local Muslim leaders to counterbalance the internal strife threatening to tear the kingdom apart from within.\n\n*Legacy and Final Years*\nThe Battle of Hattin in 1187 proved catastrophic for the Crusader forces and Raymond barely escaped the slaughter while many of his rivals perished or were captured. He retreated to Tripoli where he died shortly after the devastating defeat that allowed Saladin to capture Jerusalem and most other major cities held by the Franks. Historians debate whether his cautious strategies were correct or if they weakened the kingdom morale before the final clash. His life represents a complex chapter in Crusader history where cultural exchange and political pragmatism clashed with religious fervor and military ambition. The loss of his leadership left a vacuum that no subsequent noble could fill effectively during the decline of the Latin East. His story illustrates the difficulties faced by European nobles trying to maintain a foothold in a region increasingly united against them under capable Muslim command.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -4.7923284e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0049364017}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.023253743}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.03940214}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -9.536748e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -9.286835e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -2.6226078e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.08481045e-05}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -5.96